---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="About RUDRA" description="RUDRA's philosophy, design goals and the ecosystem around the language." path="/about">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:760px">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>A compiled language built around a native backend</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        RUDRA is a statically typed, compiled systems language. Its defining
        trait is a native backend: the compiler turns a single source file into
        a native executable directly.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.5rem">Philosophy</h2>
      <p>
        RUDRA aims to be easy to read, predictable to compile, and honest about
        its scope. The language favors a small, well-defined surface over a long
        list of partially working features. Capabilities are added when they can
        be compiled, run and verified.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">Design goals</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Readable syntax.</strong> Familiar function, binding and control-flow forms.</li>
        <li><strong>Static type checking.</strong> Type errors are caught before a program runs.</li>
        <li><strong>Memory-safety orientation.</strong> The language is designed around safe access patterns.</li>
        <li><strong>A native backend.</strong> Source compiles to a native executable.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest scope.</strong> Documented capabilities match what the shipped toolchain does.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2rem">The ecosystem</h2>
      <p>
        Around the language sits a toolchain: a driver, a compiler, a package
        tool and a language server, plus planned components such as a graphical
        IDE. Each is presented independently on the
        <a href="/ecosystem">Ecosystem</a> page with an accurate status so that
        experimental and planned work is never presented as production ready.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-info" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <h4>Two toolchains, one language</h4>
        <p>
          The publicly distributed toolchain binaries are dynamically linked
          executables. The project also has a separate self-hosting research
          compiler that is not part of this public distribution. This site only
          makes claims about the distributed release. See
          <a href="/transparency">Transparency</a> for details.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
